import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Retro: admitting census:mul_unsqueeze — the guard question, resolved","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Retro: admitting `census:mul_unsqueeze` — the guard question, resolved","type":"retro","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/mul-unsq-nat.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/mul-unsq-nat.md"}'),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/mul-unsq-nat.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="retro-admitting-census-mul-unsqueeze-—-the-guard-question-resolved" tabindex="-1">Retro: admitting <code>census:mul_unsqueeze</code> — the guard question, resolved <a class="header-anchor" href="#retro-admitting-census-mul-unsqueeze-—-the-guard-question-resolved" aria-label="Permalink to “Retro: admitting census:mul_unsqueeze — the guard question, resolved”">​</a></h1><p>Context: <code>exposed-math-discovery.json</code> recorded the exposure agent&#39;s best find — <code>mul(unsqueeze U d, unsqueeze V d) = unsqueeze(mul U V, d)</code> on <code>DeltaRuleBlock</code>: new, non-derivable, 4 typed fires, −12.27% (992496→870723), verify pass, certificate replays — blocked only by &quot;conditional truth (view-oracle): no single mechanical feature separates&quot;. This retro records the admission decision: what the guard is, why the machine-minted clause was not it, and what shipped.</p><h2 id="the-truth-region" tabindex="-1">The truth region <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-truth-region" aria-label="Permalink to “The truth region”">​</a></h2><p><code>unsq(x, d)</code> inserts a size-1 axis at <code>d mod (rank+1)</code>; broadcasting right-aligns. Pushing <code>mul</code> through a shared insertion is the naturality <code>mul(unsq u d, unsq v d) = unsq(mul u v, d)</code>. The semantic boundary is exact and mechanical:</p><ul><li><strong>equal ranks</strong> — always true (both operands insert at the same normalized position; well-typedness of either side is the same condition: <code>bcast(u,v)</code> must resolve);</li><li><strong>unequal ranks</strong> — true iff each operand&#39;s flat-read coefficient map agrees across the two orders. The shorter operand&#39;s inserted axis lands at <code>d + (R − r)</code> in the result grid vs the RHS&#39;s <code>d mod (R+1)</code>; inserts inside the broadcast pad (<code>d ≤ 0</code> region) commute, mid-rank inserts do not — e.g. <code>u=(2,), v=(1,2,2), d=1</code> equalizes the <em>grids</em> <code>(1,1,2,2)</code> both ways but reads <code>u[i]</code> vs <code>u[j]</code>.</li></ul><p>Adversarial sweep (pure IR semantics — mod-normalized dims, numpy broadcast; ranks ≤3, extents {1,2,3}, d ∈ −8..8): <strong>12956 evaluable equal sites, 2464 unequal, 1388 rhs-err</strong>. Verdicts:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>guard</th><th>bad-accepts</th><th>equal-missed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>rank-eq(u,v) ∧ shaped(bcast u v)</code></td><td>0</td><td>6156 (~48%)</td></tr><tr><td>grid-eq only — <code>bcast-eq(unsq-out u d, unsq-out v d, unsq-out(bcast u v) d, ·)</code></td><td><strong>212</strong></td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td><code>flat-map-unsq(u, UD, v, unsq-out(v, UD))</code></td><td><strong>0</strong></td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>flat-map-unsq</code> — the <code>wl</code>-naturality predicate minted for <code>mul(unsq u K, reshape v S) = unsq(mul u v, K)</code> — is the <em>exact</em> region when its partner-shape argument <code>S</code> is instantiated with the partner&#39;s own <code>unsq-out</code> spec: it checks grid coincidence AND both operands&#39; flat-read maps. That is the shipped guard — one clause, pure DSL data, serializable.</p><h2 id="what-auto-cond-minted-—-and-why-it-is-not-the-guard" tabindex="-1">What auto_cond minted — and why it is not the guard <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-auto-cond-minted-—-and-why-it-is-not-the-guard" aria-label="Permalink to “What auto_cond minted — and why it is not the guard”">​</a></h2><p><code>auto_cond_object</code> on the bare pattern (synth_limit 360, 174 measured envs: 69 equal / 16 bad) minted a single clause — the grid-eq <code>bcast-eq</code> above — separating its measured domain perfectly and <strong>unsound</strong> past it: the synthesized env bank never produced the unequal-rank mid-insert corner (the sweep shows 212 bad-accepts). The stock <code>_pred_bank</code> emits <code>flat-map-unsq</code> only with <code>reshape-out</code> partner specs — the <code>unsq-out</code> instantiation the two-sided law needs is outside the minted vocabulary, so the search picked the best <em>available</em> clause and the corner is unmeasured, not unguardable. Honest lesson for the bank: <code>flat-map-unsq</code>&#39;s <code>S</code> position should also range over <code>unsq-out</code> specs when the partner view is unsqueeze.</p><h2 id="the-sibling-mixed-mul-unsqueeze-r-id-—-already-admitted" tabindex="-1">The sibling: <code>mixed:mul_unsqueeze_r_id</code> — already admitted <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-sibling-mixed-mul-unsqueeze-r-id-—-already-admitted" aria-label="Permalink to “The sibling: mixed:mul_unsqueeze_r_id — already admitted”">​</a></h2><p><code>mul(V, unsqueeze(U,d)) → mul(U,V)</code> is α-identical to the shipped <code>mul_unsq_pad_r</code> (promoted-laws.md row 6) — the same <code>unsq:d_in_pad</code> family. Corpus-wide tally of its 22 strict-match sites / 12 models:</p><ul><li><strong>2 sites accepted by the shipped guard</strong> (intake:BroadcastPadRight, zoo:TimeCondConv) — true pad strips;</li><li><strong>10 rejected sites are numerically unequal</strong> — including all 4 DiagDenseSSM sites, where <code>V</code> binds <code>b_eye</code>: <code>I·unsq(U,−1)</code> and <code>U·I</code> coincide <em>only</em> because the identity masks off-diagonals (<code>diag(U)</code> both ways) — a contextual equality, not a law. The −14.3% &quot;pay&quot; measured there was the false direction made invisible by eye-masking; <code>unsq:d_in_pad</code> rejecting <code>d=−1</code> (trailing insert, not a pad) was the correct refusal;</li><li><strong>10 rejected sites unevaluable</strong> (err);</li><li><strong>0 rejected-but-equal</strong> — the shipped guard loses nothing.</li></ul><p>So the same guard resolution <em>already</em> unlocks it: the store object stays unshipped <em>as a store object</em> because the law it names is the law that shipped.</p><h2 id="what-shipped" tabindex="-1">What shipped <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-shipped" aria-label="Permalink to “What shipped”">​</a></h2><p><code>mul_unsq_nat</code> → <code>SIMPLIFICATION_RULES</code> (default-on; the pad-strip family&#39;s home):</p><pre><code>mul(unsq u UD, unsq v UD) -&gt; unsq(mul u v, UD)
cond = (&quot;flat-map-unsq&quot;, &quot;u&quot;, &quot;UD&quot;, &quot;v&quot;, (&quot;unsq-out&quot;, &quot;v&quot;, &quot;UD&quot;))
</code></pre><p>Closure measurement (7-model slice, lone-rule reach): zero enode growth off-site; <code>DeltaRuleBlock</code> +1.3% enodes (558→565), −12.3% cost, <code>add_cert=pass</code>, fixed-point. The default set is justified — no speculative e-node bloat, real pay on-site.</p><p><code>ALL_RULES</code> 82 → 83; <code>MACHINE_SHIPPED</code> 10 → 11; serialization census 56/56 cond-carrying rules round-trip; <code>missing_hooks == ()</code>.</p>`,19)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};